every year proud owners of vintage and  classic cars get together for countless  meets and rallies like the classic days  at dyke palace  they've polished and tinkered all winter  and can hardly wait to hit the road  but all too often vintage car owners  value looks more than safety and tires  are far down the maintenance list but  the tires put the rubber to the road and  take enormous strain often for much  longer than is safe  the potential consequences can be  demonstrated on a test track  porsche regularly puts tires of all  makes through their paces tires for  their newest generations and for the  many older porsches on the road it's the  only car maker to do so  porsche test driver dieter rush eisen  says this car has older tires on it with  only seven millimeters of tread  some think that's enough but it's not  every year a tire loses more traction  that's very noticeable in wet conditions  when the front axle can't hold the car's  direction  in a curve the car goes forward and  can't be steered  the only way to guide the car is to let  up on the gas to regain some control on  the front axle  again  the wide steering angle that results can  lead to disaster if the rear axle loses  traction  the driver won't be able to compensate  through the steering wheel  a wipeout is close to inevitable  and even beyond that as soon as the  driver hits the brakes the wheels lock  older vehicles don't have anti-lock  braking systems so the driver has to be  gentle with the brakes  even then the front axle may still lock  up then the car will skid in the  direction it was going  a tire has to meet the highest standards  to be porsche certified it has to meet  33 criteria 100 percent that applies to  tires for new as well as for older  models  porsche's goals are the greatest  possible customer satisfaction and best  driving experience  porsche approved tires are marked with  an n their website also lists approved  summer and winter tires for both current  and vintage models  the approved tires usually cost about  the same  porsche test driver detox eisen says  once the new tires are on they provide  the needed traction and the car is  noticeably faster  in terms of lap times that means 18  seconds less for 1900 meters on the wet  handling track  the cars are not only faster but safer  too  the traction gives more directional  control to the front axle and the wheels  don't lock up when the driver hits the  brakes  it makes an enormous difference now the  handling is much quicker and more  responsive there's a margin for safety  that wasn't there before  it's good before i light and eat  good tires put the fun back in driving  many vintage car owners only head out in  nice weather if at all  sometimes their tires are years old then  if they take part in a rally or classic  car event and it rains it's a disaster  waiting to happen  all too often the driver only starts  thinking about what should have been  done after the car is in a ditch  to make sure that doesn't happen porsche  recommends new tires every six or seven  years no matter how much tread they have  even with vintage cars safety is the  bottom line
 

